{
  "0": "Training strategy: across all four datasets, change only the learning-rate schedule: retain the current initial rate for five epochs, then multiply it by max(0.25, 0.98^(epoch-5)). Gentler late updates may reduce the observed validation oscillations while preserving early learning. Keep the optimizer, batch size, dropout, weight decay, model, loss, sampling, stopping policy and 200-epoch cap unchanged; train one model per category.",
  "1": "Learning objective: across all four datasets, use L = 0.9*L_existing + 0.1*mean(softplus(s_negative-s_positive)) over existing valid positive-negative training pairs. Reuse exactly the current sampled negatives and scores. This modest pairwise term may improve candidate ordering, particularly in Electronics, without discarding the existing prediction objective. Preserve architecture, readout, sampling and all training settings; retain one checkpoint per category.",
  "2": "Feature construction: across all four datasets, compute item counts c_i solely from training interactions and their median m over observed items. Multiply existing input embeddings by 1+0.1*tanh(log1p(m)-log1p(c_i)); keep padding zero. This bounded rarity feature may reduce popular-item dominance and expose less frequent preferences without new learned parameters or score fusion. Save the count-derived scales and apply them identically in the predictor writer. Preserve GRU, readout, loss and optimizer.",
  "3": "Sequence modeling: across all four datasets, cap each causal input context at min(current_max_length, 30), retaining the most recent interactions in their original chronological order. Apply the same truncation in training and generated prediction code while retaining every existing training target. Shorter contexts may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation. Preserve features, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, loss, optimizer, batch settings and stopping policy; use one model per category."
}
